Docker is an open-source containerization platform used to package applications and their dependencies into lightweight, portable containers.
A container provides an isolated environment in which an application can execute consistently across different systems. Unlike virtual machines, containers share the host operating system kernel, making them lightweight and efficient.

The main components of Docker are:
- Docker Image: A read-only template used to create containers.
- Docker Container: A running instance of a Docker image.
- Dockerfile: A text file containing instructions to build a Docker image.
- Docker Engine: The software responsible for building and running containers.
- Docker Hub: A registry used to store and share container images.
Docker simplifies application deployment, improves portability, and ensures consistency between development and production environments.


Procedure:

Install Docker Desktop and start the Docker service.
Verify the installation using docker --version.
Create a Dockerfile for a sample application.
Build a Docker image using docker build.
Run the container using docker run.
Verify the running container using docker ps.
